  "summary": "Zebrafish and Oikopleura preprints and a published Arctic cod study report intra-specific variation in responses to sound.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these items is intra-specific variation in how aquatic organisms respond to sound. In zebrafish, emergence latency differed among individuals and decreased across two tests, and sound exposure reduced vertical midline crossings more than ambient sound did. In Oikopleura dioica, anthropogenic noise altered behaviour in a life stage- and signal-dependent manner, with larvae appearing most sensitive. In Arctic cod, auditory evoked potential measurements revealed seasonal differences in hearing sensitivity between spawning and non-spawning fish. What is new is the explicit treatment of individual, life-stage and seasonal variation as features of sound responses, rather than a single fixed response. The Arctic cod study is the only published item here, while the zebrafish and Oikopleura work are preprints and should be treated as provisional.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
